Material delta: promote ROADWORK =E2=86=92 BUS OPERATIONAL OUTCOME to PLANT
NOW. This is the cleanest fully-open causal OutcomeGraph found this
run because both sides are official, event-rich and already share
strong spatiotemporal structure.

Street Manager exposes every utility/local-authority roadwork in
England and includes event_reference, work_reference_number,
permit_reference_number, coordinates, USRN, proposed/actual start/end,
work status, traffic-management type, event_time, object_reference and
version. Its current Event API additionally exposes GET
/works/updates, POST /permits/search and notification
subscribe/unsubscribe endpoints. The data is public/free under OGL,
although an API account is required.

BODS exposes timetable plus live SIRI-VM vehicle positions for local
buses in England. The national vehicle feed can be requested as often
as every 5 seconds; operators are expected to update vehicle locations
every 10=E2=80=9330 seconds. BODS data is explicitly open for
commercial/non-commercial reuse with attribution, with a free consumer
account/API key.

Therefore POW can build:
ROADWORK(event_reference, USRN, geometry, traffic_management,
actual_start/end, version)
=E2=86=92 affected route/stop corridor
=E2=86=92 BUS(vehicle_journey, scheduled progress, observed progress)
=E2=86=92 delay / traversal-time / missing-service state
=E2=86=92 roadwork end
=E2=86=92 recovery curve.

This is much stronger than a static roadworks graph. The proprietary
object is the measured causal response of physical mobility to
specific interventions.

