JOIN-QUALITY METRICS

WorkIdentityContinuity =3D % event/object references surviving updates
without ambiguous remap.
USRNCoverage =3D % works with usable USRN.
RouteExposurePrecision =3D sampled fraction of inferred affected
journeys that physically intersect work geometry.
JourneyIdentityCoverage =3D % observed SIRI-VM journeys resolvable to
timetable/service identity.
ObservationCompleteness =3D expected vs received vehicle observations.
TreatmentContamination =3D fraction of affected route-time windows with
overlapping roadworks/incidents.
CounterfactualCoverage =3D % treated journeys with usable
same-route/same-time historical or matched-control baseline.
OutcomeConfidence must combine identity confidence + spatial exposure
confidence + telemetry completeness + counterfactual quality.

FIRST TRANSFORMATIONS

DelayDelta(work, journey, t) =3D ObservedJourneyTime - ExpectedJourneyTime.

RoadworkImpact(work) =3D E[delay | exposed during work] - E[delay |
matched untreated baseline].

RecoveryHalfLife(work) =3D time after actual_end until route delay
returns halfway from peak degradation to baseline.

TrafficManagementImpact(type, road_class, place, time) =3D posterior
distribution of delay induced by closure/lights/lane restriction/etc.

The last transformation becomes an agent-action primitive: given a
proposed work, forecast likely operational disruption and choose
timing/traffic-management alternatives.

OUTCOME ACQUISITION

Public/open outcome route is unusually strong here: BODS live vehicle
state supplies observed physical consequences without customer PII.
Store only public operational vehicle/journey identifiers needed for
transport analysis; do not infer driver identity.

Later first-party outcome routes: consenting bus operators/local
authorities can provide actual control interventions, cancellations,
passenger impacts or internal cost. These are enrichment, not required
to plant the base garden.

PLANT NOW =E2=80=94 SMALLEST COVERAGE TEST

Scope: one English urban authority, 24 hours.
1. Register for Street Manager API access and BODS consumer access.
2. Subscribe to Street Manager notifications if production entitlement
supports it; otherwise reconcile /works/updates.
3. Preserve raw work event versions append-only.
4. Poll BODS at 30-second cadence only for routes/stops intersecting
active work buffers where filtering permits; otherwise capture the
documented central response at a conservative cadence.
5. Build affected-corridor edges using USRN + geometry.
6. Resolve BODS vehicle journeys to schedules.
7. Calculate per-journey deviation before/during/after work.

1-DAY GATES:
- Street Manager stable work identity >=3D98%
- USRN or usable geometry >=3D95%
- BODS journey resolution >=3D90%
- telemetry completeness >=3D90% in sampled treatment windows
- at least 10 work =C3=97 journey exposure windows

1-WEEK GATES:
- >=3D100 treatment windows
- >=3D70% have credible matched baseline
- manually reviewed exposure precision >=3D90%
- repeated works/traffic-management classes show measurable
heterogeneous response

KILL THRESHOLD:
Kill the causal product if route exposure cannot exceed 80% precision
or telemetry/baseline quality prevents stable effect estimates. Do NOT
kill raw event/version preservation while acquisition remains cheap,
because work-version history itself is useful and live bus telemetry
is difficult to recreate at full resolution later.

RECONSTRUCTION TEST
Street Manager current/history: some history exists, but exact
event/version chronology plus synchronized bus state is not guaranteed
to remain cheaply reconstructable.
BODS annual/historic punctuality: exists, but it is not equivalent to
preserving the raw 10=E2=80=9330-second vehicle state aligned to a specific
work intervention.
Therefore the joined intervention tape PASSES the time-moat test even
though each source individually has historical products.

VEHICLE/MOT SECONDARY JOIN
DVSA's current MOT History API endpoint is
https://history.mot.api.gov.uk with registration and VIN lookups plus
bulk download. It provides MOT tests and defects and is available to
registered organisations/businesses/individuals after approval. This
gives a strong longitudinal vehicle outcome key, especially VIN where
available.
Potential graph: VIN/registration =E2=86=92 MOT defect =E2=86=92 repair
recommendation/receipt [partner] =E2=86=92 next MOT =E2=86=92
recurrence/non-recurrence.
The public MOT history is largely backfillable, so MOT itself is JOIN
INFRASTRUCTURE, not the garden. The garden begins when POW records its
own recommended intervention/part/repairer/cost and observes the next
test/repair outcome.

COUNTEREVIDENCE
- Roadworks correlate with congestion and may not cause all observed
delay; weather, incidents, school calendars and unrelated congestion
must enter counterfactual matching.
- USRN identifies a street, not necessarily the precise lane/direction
traversed by a route; geometry is required.
- BODS feed gaps can masquerade as operational degradation. Separate
TELEMETRY_MISSING from SERVICE_MISSING.
- Operators may divert buses around works; a route deviation is itself
an outcome but breaks naive scheduled-corridor matching.
- Existing commercial services already normalize Street Manager. POW's
moat therefore cannot be roadwork normalization; it must be
longitudinal causal outcome learning.

